\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Preference comparison and preference data law}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{preference}
\tag{feedback}

\p{For a prompt #{x}, a \strong{preference comparison} is an ordered triple
#{(x,y^+,y^-)}. The response #{y^+} was selected over #{y^-} by a declared
judge under a declared comparison rule. A \strong{preference data law}
#{\mathcal D_{\mathrm{pref}}} is a probability law on such triples.}

\p{The order records an observation. It does not by itself provide a
cardinal utility, transitivity, or agreement among judges.}

\p{Pairwise comparisons enter reward learning in
\citet{Sec. 2}{christiano2017deep}. Their use in language-model post-training
appears in \citet{Sec. 3.3}{ouyang2022training}. FTIP keeps the judge and
acquisition rule visible because they determine what information the law
contains.}
